    "summary": "High‑energy set with killer Cryptical > Other One and solid blues jams",
    "blurb": "The show bursts into life with a fiery Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One transition and tight Good Lovin' drumming. Mid‑set blues highlights include Cumberland Blues and I'm A King Bee, while the encore delivers Uncle John's Band and Dancin' In The Street.",
    "review": "On March 1, 1970 at the Family Dog on the Great Highway, the Dead delivered a high‑octane performance that still feels fresh. The first set opens with classic rockers like New Speedway Boogie and Casey Jones before launching into a memorable Cryptical Envelopment → Drums → The Other One → Cryptical Envelopment sequence that reviewers call “exciting” and comparable to the New Orleans show a month earlier. Jerry’s guitar work shines, even if a touch louder would have been ideal, while Pigpen’s harmonica adds a gritty edge throughout. The rhythm section—Mickey, Bill, and Phil—holds down a tight groove, especially on Good Lovin', which is highlighted for its powerful drumming.\n\nThe middle of the set settles into bluesy territory with standout renditions of Cumberland Blues, I’m A King Bee, and the seamless China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider pairing. Despite a few level spikes in the early audience‑sourced tracks, the soundboard capture is generally clear and well‑balanced. The encore rounds out the night with a heartfelt Uncle John's Band, an upbeat Dancin' In The Street, and a tender close on It’s All Over Now Baby Blue, making this show a must‑listen for fans of the early‑70s Dead energy.",
